    "case_id": "133",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old man presented with 1 week of difficulty voiding and was catheterized for urinary retention. Ultrasonography revealed a large prostatic mass, and his prostate-specific antigen level was 0.62 ng/mL (normal <4.0). He denied pain, fever, or abdominal distension. Physical examination was unremarkable, and complete blood count, routine chemistries, and tumor markers (CEA, CA19-9, CA-125, SCC antigen, CYFRA) were all within normal limits. Digital rectal examination revealed an enlarged prostate with palpable nodules and an ill-defined border. Pelvic ultrasound confirmed a heterogeneous prostatic mass. Magnetic resonance imaging showed a 5.5-cm heterogeneous lesion in the right prostate lobe, and cystoscopy raised suspicion for bladder invasion. Positron emission tomography–CT and colonoscopy suggested rectal invasion. A provisional diagnosis of prostatic sarcoma was made, and a transrectal needle biopsy was performed. The biopsy result was interpreted as either prostatic stromal sarcoma or prostatic stromal tumor of uncertain malignant potential.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. ProstateStromalSarcoma\nB. Stromal tumor of uncertain malignant potential was less likely once invasive growth was seen on histology\nC. Gastrointestinal stromal tumor was excluded by lack of c\nD. Solitary fibrous tumor was excluded by absence of stat6 nuclear positivity\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
